The Materials Science behind Sustainable Metals and Alloys — Dierk Raabe (@mat-sustainable-metals: 0, is #reference, #part-of @mat-sources) { r_6MXWN12M466MS374XY585GBHBN
Dierk Raabe. *The Materials Science behind Sustainable Metals and Alloys*. Chemical Reviews 123 (2023), 2436–2608, DOI 10.1021/acs.chemrev.2c00799. [Author-hosted full review](https://www.dierk-raabe.com/app/download/5818255182/acs.chemrev.2c00799_with_volume_ID_s.pdf).

Read the complete review alongside the UNEP recycling book. Use sections 6.3–6.4 for secondary/tertiary feed, especially impurity inheritance and multiple-metal recovery, then the process and alloy-design sections when comparing interventions. Follow the primary methods behind a selected claim rather than treating a review as the experiment itself.

Title, contents, introduction and selected sections 6.3.4 and 6.3.11 were inspected on 30 October 2026. Their examples connect composition and separation with subsequent processing; numerical impurity tolerances remain alloy/process dependent. Sector figures and proposed developments are dated evidence.

The author supplies the full text. A blanket adaptation licence was not verified, and individual figures have their own credits. Keep it external. Neither a mass recovery percentage nor the presence of a recyclable element establishes retained microstructure or a qualified product.

